      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "44899dcc-5845-56c8-a18c-dfef05cfba60",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- Places: Doggerland, southern North Sea, Great Britain, continental Europe, North Atlantic Ocean\n- Events: last glacial period, Mesolithic period, Storegga Slide, tsunami\n- Works: Stone Spring, Northland\n- Concepts: rising sea levels, human habitation\n\nFacts:\n- Doggerland was an area beneath what is now the southern North Sea that connected Great Britain to continental Europe during and after the last glacial period.\n- Doggerland was probably a rich habitat with human habitation during the Mesolithic period.\n- Rising sea levels gradually reduced Doggerland to low-lying islands before its final submergence.\n- Doggerland may have finally submerged after a tsunami caused by the Storegga Slide.\n- The three Storegga Slides are considered among the largest known landslides.\n- The Storegga Slide collapse involved about 290 km of coastal shelf and about 3500 km3 of debris, causing a very large tsunami in the North Atlantic Ocean.\n- Stone Spring is set in prehistoric Doggerland, which the novel renames Northland.\n- Stone Spring focuses on Northland's inhabitants trying to adapt to rising sea levels that slowly erode Northland's coastline.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "eb972407-f63a-541f-815d-6f6d52897715",
        "text": "Page: Doggerland. Sentence: Doggerland was an area now beneath the southern North Sea that connected Great Britain to continental Europe during and after the last glacial period.\nPage: Doggerland. Sentence: It was probably a rich habitat with human habitation in the Mesolithic period, although rising sea levels gradually reduced it to low-lying islands before its final submergence, possibly following a tsunami caused by the Storegga Slide.\nPage: Storegga Slide. Sentence: The three Storegga Slides are considered to be amongst the largest known landslides.\nPage: Storegga Slide. Sentence: The collapse involved an estimated 290 km length of coastal shelf, with a total volume of 3500 km3 of debris, which caused a very large tsunami in the North Atlantic Ocean.\nPage: Stone Spring. Sentence: It is set in prehistoric Doggerland (renamed \"Northland\" in the novel) and focuses on the attempts of Northland's inhabitants to adapt to the rising sea levels slowly eroding Northland's coastline.",
        "type": "DocumentChunk"
      },
